 ],
 "literal": "In the tree-crowded forest where flocks of peacocks call / where grey-faced black monkeys shiver with their young ones / in the country of the lord of the slopes where heavy rain poured / I looked at his hill, O friend! / Look at my forehead, is it like it was in the old days?",
 "translation": "In the tree-dense forest where flocks of peacocks call, and grey-faced black monkeys shiver with their young as heavy rains pour on the mountain slopes of his land, I looked at his hill, my friend. Look at my forehead now! Has it not returned to its beautiful old state?",
 "modern": "மயில்களின் கூட்டங்கள் கூவுகின்ற, மரங்கள் நிறைந்த காட்டில், நரைத்த முகத்தைக் கொண்ட கருங்குரங்குகள் தம் குட்டிகளுடன் நடுங்கும்படி பெருமழை பொழியும் மலைச்சாரலை உடைய தலைவனின் நாட்டு மலையை நான் பார்த்தேன், தோழி! என் நெற்றியைப் பார்; அது முன்பு இருந்ததைப் போலப் பசலை நீங்கி அழகாக மாறிவிட்டது அல்லவா!",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend after catching sight of her lover's mountain. She explains that her deep distress and the paleness (pasalai) on her face have completely vanished just by looking at his hill. The cold, wet mountain slopes of the Kuriñci landscape are described with shivering monkeys and calling peacocks, representing the heavy rains of the season. This imagery reflects the protective and nourishing presence of the lover, whose very land brings warmth and life back to the heroine. Her forehead, which had lost its luster during their separation, has now regained its former beauty.",
